<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rinchen Zangpo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">958–1055</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rinchen Zangpo was a Tibetan translator and teacher who led the revival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> by the kings of Guge, he translated many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> texts into Tibetan and brought back Kashmiri artists. He is credited with founding many temples in Guge, Ladakh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiti">Spiti</a>, including Tabo, and helped prepare the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atisha">Atisha</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rinchen-zangpo.jpg" alt="Rinchen Zangpo" width="131" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: www.treasuryoflives.org · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richen_Zangpo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
